WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.280 --> 00:00:05.570
Speaker 1: It's great to welcome to the program
today, Bill Browder, who is the CEO of Hermitage

00:00:05.570 --> 00:00:12.769
Capital Management, head of the Global Magnitsky
Justice Campaign, and also author of the book

00:00:12.769 --> 00:00:13.769
Red Notice.

00:00:13.769 --> 00:00:21.360
So, Bill, to start with, the the story you
tell in your book includes, as you got into

00:00:21.360 --> 00:00:26.790
the world of finance, your interest in Eastern
Europe, which included initially doing some

00:00:26.790 --> 00:00:33.079
work in Poland when there was relatively little
work being done in Poland by U.S. and British

00:00:33.079 --> 00:00:39.449
based folks in the finance world and eventually
actually going to Russia itself.

00:00:39.449 --> 00:00:43.629
Talk a little bit about how that all worked
and how you ended up working in that part

00:00:43.629 --> 00:00:44.629
of the world.

00:00:44.629 --> 00:00:45.629
Speaker 4: Sure.

00:00:45.629 --> 00:00:55.929
So I went out to Russia in 1992, just at the
beginning of the Russian privatization program,

00:00:55.929 --> 00:01:03.260
basically when when when the Soviet Union
broke up, Russia became a sort of, quote,

00:01:03.260 --> 00:01:04.390
democracy.

00:01:04.390 --> 00:01:07.180
The new president wanted to go from communism
to capitalism.

00:01:07.180 --> 00:01:12.010
And in order to do that, they basically the
state owned all the property and they said,

00:01:12.010 --> 00:01:17.270
we're going to give all the property away
for free to the people and make them capitalists.

00:01:17.270 --> 00:01:23.020
And and, of course, as we know, most of the
property ended up in the hands of a very small

00:01:23.020 --> 00:01:25.020
number of Russian oligarchs.

00:01:25.020 --> 00:01:27.860
But there were little crumbs falling off the
table.

00:01:27.860 --> 00:01:31.460
And those crumbs were shares of some of the
biggest Russian companies which are trading

00:01:31.460 --> 00:01:33.500
on the stock market.

00:01:33.500 --> 00:01:38.960
And I went and set up a business called the
Hermitage Fund to basically pick up those

00:01:38.960 --> 00:01:43.609
crumbs and invest in the newly privatized
companies.

00:01:43.609 --> 00:01:51.350
And for a while, it was just a bonanza, like
bigger profits, I guess, you know, made cryptocurrency

00:01:51.350 --> 00:01:56.509
gains look like child's play was just so the
returns were so great.

00:01:56.509 --> 00:02:03.840
Speaker 1: So you eventually got involved
in this space where there was the possibility

00:02:03.840 --> 00:02:12.890
of investing in Russian companies at massive
discounts from what traditional finance measures

00:02:12.890 --> 00:02:16.560
would assess as the value of these companies.

00:02:16.560 --> 00:02:20.480
Talk a little bit about how you how that worked,
how you got into that.

00:02:20.480 --> 00:02:26.780
Speaker 4: Well, so the situation was that
that nobody really understood what was there.

00:02:26.780 --> 00:02:29.060
It was sort of a state secret.

00:02:29.060 --> 00:02:31.120
The oil industry was a state secret.

00:02:31.120 --> 00:02:36.670
You know, all these big companies were not
known to the Russians.

00:02:36.670 --> 00:02:38.960
And the Russians had these these vouchers.

00:02:38.960 --> 00:02:43.310
They call them privatization certificates,
and they could invest their vouchers and whatever

00:02:43.310 --> 00:02:46.400
they wanted to invest them in or sell their
vouchers.

00:02:46.400 --> 00:02:51.180
And most Russians didn't really think that
their vouchers would be worth anything.

00:02:51.180 --> 00:02:55.780
And so they sold them for a bottle of vodka
and and various people were buying up vouchers.

00:02:55.780 --> 00:02:59.080
And this voucher exchange exchanges formed.

00:02:59.080 --> 00:03:05.239
And and I did the math and I said, OK, if
you buy shares and some of these oil companies,

00:03:05.239 --> 00:03:09.989
you're buying oil and like 10 cents per barrel
of oil reserves in the ground.

00:03:09.989 --> 00:03:15.440
And at the time, oil prices were, let's say,
trading at thirty dollars and so 10 cents

00:03:15.440 --> 00:03:18.849
on the ground versus thirty dollars out of
the ground, that that kind of had to be a

00:03:18.849 --> 00:03:20.370
good deal.

00:03:20.370 --> 00:03:27.030
And so I tell you the truth, it was it was
really hard to get people interested in the

00:03:27.030 --> 00:03:30.319
West in that I would say, look, you know,
these things are trading in a ninety nine

00:03:30.319 --> 00:03:32.470
point nine percent discount.

00:03:32.470 --> 00:03:35.599
And most people, when they heard the word
Russia, they ran for the hills.

00:03:35.599 --> 00:03:42.690
And so it wasn't like this was something that
was like a slam dunk obvious to most people.

00:03:42.690 --> 00:03:46.120
Most people said, well, you know, whatever
the valuations are, I don't want to go anywhere

00:03:46.120 --> 00:03:47.120
near Russia.

00:03:47.120 --> 00:03:56.140
Speaker 1: So ultimately, you fell out of
favor in Russia and ultimately were even expelled

00:03:56.140 --> 00:03:57.420
from the country.

00:03:57.420 --> 00:03:58.790
Legal problems.

00:03:58.790 --> 00:04:00.709
What what went wrong?

00:04:00.709 --> 00:04:02.420
How did the problem start to develop?

00:04:02.420 --> 00:04:08.970
Speaker 4: Well, so the companies that I ended
up becoming the largest foreign investor in

00:04:08.970 --> 00:04:12.829
Russia, we had four and a half billion dollars
invested in all these companies.

00:04:12.829 --> 00:04:17.650
And what I discovered was all the companies
were basically being robbed blind by these

00:04:17.650 --> 00:04:20.620
oligarchs who were the majority shareholders.

00:04:20.620 --> 00:04:28.160
And so I decided to try to start fighting
the thievery that the robbing of these companies

00:04:28.160 --> 00:04:29.640
by exposing it.

00:04:29.640 --> 00:04:38.919
And in doing so, you know, in these dictatorships
and third world countries, business and politics

00:04:38.919 --> 00:04:40.400
are all sort of intertwined.

00:04:40.400 --> 00:04:46.270
So the moment that I started exposing all
these nasty things going on, all of a sudden

00:04:46.270 --> 00:04:49.759
the politics got involved and I was expelled
from the country.

00:04:49.759 --> 00:04:51.699
I was declared a threat to national security.

00:04:51.699 --> 00:04:53.300
My offices were raided.

00:04:53.300 --> 00:04:55.250
They seized all of our documents.

00:04:55.250 --> 00:04:59.240
And the next thing we knew, those documents
were used to.

00:04:59.240 --> 00:05:05.069
Perpetrator, a highly complex fraud, where
they then stole our companies and used those

00:05:05.069 --> 00:05:09.180
companies to steal two hundred and thirty
million dollars of taxes that my company had

00:05:09.180 --> 00:05:10.710
paid to the Russian government.

00:05:10.710 --> 00:05:16.569
And so it was all politics, mixed with business,
mixed with criminality.

00:05:16.569 --> 00:05:19.130
And I was on the receiving end of all of that
stuff.

00:05:19.130 --> 00:05:20.130
Speaker 1: Right.

00:05:20.130 --> 00:05:26.470
So people may or may not know, but Russia
is a huge country.

00:05:26.470 --> 00:05:34.050
It has extremely rural areas and those rural
areas have courts.

00:05:34.050 --> 00:05:42.720
And often those courts are operating under
the direction of who knows who in power, bribes,

00:05:42.720 --> 00:05:44.380
corruption, etc..

00:05:44.380 --> 00:05:45.880
Talk a little bit about how that all worked.

00:05:45.880 --> 00:05:51.230
I mean, that that was really the basis for
how these assets and companies were taken

00:05:51.230 --> 00:05:52.230
from you.

00:05:52.230 --> 00:05:53.990
Speaker 4: Yes, the Russians are very formalistic
plates.

00:05:53.990 --> 00:06:01.160
So so it's not like, I don't know, in Mexico
or I don't know where there's grab your stuff.

00:06:01.160 --> 00:06:03.460
And that's and that's done in Russia.

00:06:03.460 --> 00:06:07.250
Everything is sort of done with some kind
of pretense.

00:06:07.250 --> 00:06:11.350
And so they'll go to the court and the court,
just the judge will make a decision to transfer

00:06:11.350 --> 00:06:17.270
your assets to somebody else and you'll stamp
the stamp, the judgment.

00:06:17.270 --> 00:06:23.810
And and so they love all this sort of procedural
bureaucratic stuff, even though you can just

00:06:23.810 --> 00:06:27.510
unwind it and show that it's all been done
fraudulently.

00:06:27.510 --> 00:06:31.190
And for some reason, that that doesn't even
if you can show has done fraudulent, that

00:06:31.190 --> 00:06:32.190
doesn't bother them.

00:06:32.190 --> 00:06:36.319
They can all just waive their court decisions
in the air based on a five hundred dollar

00:06:36.319 --> 00:06:37.710
bribe they made to the judge.

00:06:37.710 --> 00:06:41.889
And that's the order of magnitude, the bribes
that they probably had to make in order to

00:06:41.889 --> 00:06:44.360
get these kinds of decisions through the courts.

00:06:44.360 --> 00:06:48.539
Speaker 1: So you tell one story.

00:06:48.539 --> 00:06:53.690
And the way I like to sort of when I when
I read a nonfiction book about something I'm

00:06:53.690 --> 00:06:57.009
not already familiar with, I first don't look
up anything about it.

00:06:57.009 --> 00:06:59.150
I just read the book and then I'll look up.

00:06:59.150 --> 00:07:01.170
Well, what are the other perspectives about
it?

00:07:01.170 --> 00:07:05.720
And after reading your book and hearing your
story, did doing some research as well for

00:07:05.720 --> 00:07:12.069
this interview, I come across allegations
that you were not actually the victim, but

00:07:12.069 --> 00:07:13.910
you actually were the perpetrator.

00:07:13.910 --> 00:07:19.500
In other words, you use the systems that you
criticize and say that victimized you to actually

00:07:19.500 --> 00:07:22.620
participate in the corruption and in the fraud.

00:07:22.620 --> 00:07:26.210
Now, it's beyond my ability to adjudicate
these things independently.

00:07:26.210 --> 00:07:28.810
In the context of this interview, you tell
one story.

00:07:28.810 --> 00:07:31.500
There are others who tell another story.

00:07:31.500 --> 00:07:35.440
But what do you make of the fact that there
are these counter allegations that have been

00:07:35.440 --> 00:07:38.440
made that say you are actually the perpetrator
here, are not the victim?

00:07:38.440 --> 00:07:43.599
Speaker 4: Well, I mean, first of all, those
allegations were made by the people who stole

00:07:43.599 --> 00:07:45.389
the money.

00:07:45.389 --> 00:07:52.490
And so so we have we have a and just so you
know, in in Russia, this is what they do.

00:07:52.490 --> 00:07:57.460
They shoot down a plane going over Russia
and they blame it on me, on the people who

00:07:57.460 --> 00:08:03.160
are on the plane or I mean, everything is
the other guys did it.

00:08:03.160 --> 00:08:11.639
And in our case, it's the whole thing is sort
of completely absurd from top to bottom.

00:08:11.639 --> 00:08:19.120
The money was stolen in so so our companies
were stolen and all of these sort of messianic

00:08:19.120 --> 00:08:27.090
machinations were done in 2000, in December,
like October, December of twenty seven.

00:08:27.090 --> 00:08:32.010
And we filed a criminal complaint with with
all the major Russian law enforcement agencies

00:08:32.010 --> 00:08:36.200
after we learned about the theft of our companies.

00:08:36.200 --> 00:08:38.820
And we put that onto the record.

00:08:38.820 --> 00:08:44.910
And three weeks after we filed the complaint,
the tax money was stolen, the two hundred

00:08:44.910 --> 00:08:47.350
and thirty million dollars of taxes that we
paid.

00:08:47.350 --> 00:08:53.700
And so what what criminals would file a complaint
about their upcoming crime before they committed

00:08:53.700 --> 00:08:54.700
it?

00:08:54.700 --> 00:08:58.870
I mean, there are so many errors in their
story.

00:08:58.870 --> 00:09:05.170
They also blame the story that they say that
that Sergei Magnitsky, my dead lawyer who

00:09:05.170 --> 00:09:09.321
was killed, by the way, for disclosing this,
they say he stole the money and he did it

00:09:09.321 --> 00:09:11.170
together with another dead guy.

00:09:11.170 --> 00:09:15.240
And the other dead guy died two, two months
before the crime was committed.

00:09:15.240 --> 00:09:19.720
And so, I mean, just, you know, these guys
were like Keystone cops when it comes to coming

00:09:19.720 --> 00:09:22.230
up with a conspiracy theory to blame the other
guy.

00:09:22.230 --> 00:09:29.050
Speaker 1: So there have been multiple red
notices placed against you.

00:09:29.050 --> 00:09:36.040
For people who don't know what a red notices,
a red notice is sort of a global Interpol

00:09:36.040 --> 00:09:40.020
arrest warrant or arrest directive in a sense.

00:09:40.020 --> 00:09:46.160
And there were ultimately Interpol decided,
as you tell in the book, not to enforce that

00:09:46.160 --> 00:09:47.160
red notice.

00:09:47.160 --> 00:09:51.180
They decided this is politically motivated,
this is bogus, and they decided not to enforce

00:09:51.180 --> 00:09:52.200
it.

00:09:52.200 --> 00:09:56.840
But there was a period of time where, as far
as you knew, if you had an interaction with

00:09:56.840 --> 00:10:02.240
customs or border authorities in any number
of countries, you could have been arrested

00:10:02.240 --> 00:10:03.490
and then extradited to Russia.

00:10:03.490 --> 00:10:05.760
And then who knows what would have happened.

00:10:05.760 --> 00:10:10.770
How long was that period of time where that
was a concern for you or you didn't know what

00:10:10.770 --> 00:10:11.770
would happen?

00:10:11.770 --> 00:10:18.760
Speaker 4: Well, so the book I finished reading
the book in 2014 and the first Interpol notice

00:10:18.760 --> 00:10:21.460
from Russia came in 2013.

00:10:21.460 --> 00:10:22.850
The second one came in 2013.

00:10:22.850 --> 00:10:29.830
And since the book was written, there have
been eight Interpol notices for my arrest

00:10:29.830 --> 00:10:31.040
from Russia.

00:10:31.040 --> 00:10:36.001
And so when you say how long, there's been
numerous periods of time a week here, two

00:10:36.001 --> 00:10:41.371
weeks there, three weeks where where if I
cross the border, I would be arrested, sent

00:10:41.371 --> 00:10:43.590
to Russia to my death.

00:10:43.590 --> 00:10:45.780
And I was arrested.

00:10:45.780 --> 00:10:53.010
In May of 2013, I was traveling to Madrid
to meet with the Spanish anti-corruption prosecutor

00:10:53.010 --> 00:10:58.890
to share information about Russian money laundering
connected to the murder of my lawyer, Sergei

00:10:58.890 --> 00:11:00.540
Magnitsky.

00:11:00.540 --> 00:11:05.720
And as I was walking out of my hotel room
before my meeting with the Spanish prosecutor

00:11:05.720 --> 00:11:11.680
to Spanish national policemen arrested me
on a Russian Interpol warrant.

00:11:11.680 --> 00:11:18.820
And it was only because I was allowed to pack
my bags in my hotel room before they took

00:11:18.820 --> 00:11:24.540
me away, that I tweeted out that I was being
arrested and then all hell broke loose with

00:11:24.540 --> 00:11:30.650
journalists and activists and everybody else
calling Interpol, calling the Spanish government,

00:11:30.650 --> 00:11:35.120
calling everybody else so that by the time
I got to the police station, I was released.

00:11:35.120 --> 00:11:39.450
But this kind of stuff is pretty scary and
it continues to wake me.

00:11:39.450 --> 00:11:41.240
Up until today, the Russians are after me.

00:11:41.240 --> 00:11:46.890
They've issued they've found me guilty twice
in their courts in absentia and have sent

00:11:46.890 --> 00:11:49.250
me sends me to 18 years in prison.

00:11:49.250 --> 00:11:51.870
I'd be killed if I went back there, no question.

00:11:51.870 --> 00:11:52.870
Speaker 1: Yeah.

00:11:52.870 --> 00:11:58.360
You you know, what's interesting is you talk
in the book about how something that Vladimir

00:11:58.360 --> 00:12:04.180
Putin will do sometimes when he's asked about
individuals that are his adversaries and this

00:12:04.180 --> 00:12:06.410
this happened with you.

00:12:06.410 --> 00:12:07.870
He won't say the person's name.

00:12:07.870 --> 00:12:11.890
And so there was an event you describe in
the book where Vladimir Putin was asked about

00:12:11.890 --> 00:12:17.280
you and he referred to you, I think, as like
the gentleman you're referring to when I was

00:12:17.280 --> 00:12:24.890
watching recently, the Biden Putin press conference
that took place after their meeting and Putin

00:12:24.890 --> 00:12:30.080
was asked about Navalny by a journalist, it
was almost verbatim what was said to you.

00:12:30.080 --> 00:12:37.700
Putin didn't say his name and said something
like the person you're referring to, I believe,

00:12:37.700 --> 00:12:42.210
has committed crimes in our country, which
was I mean, that's essentially the same language

00:12:42.210 --> 00:12:43.350
you describe, is it not?

00:12:43.350 --> 00:12:47.600
Speaker 4: But he used that language about
me on a number of occasions.

00:12:47.600 --> 00:12:50.160
But but here's here's the interesting thing.

00:12:50.160 --> 00:12:55.050
So after the Magnitsky Act was passed and
just for your listeners, the Magnitsky Act

00:12:55.050 --> 00:12:57.400
is a piece of legislation named after my lawyer.

00:12:57.400 --> 00:13:00.540
Sergei Magnitsky was murdered after uncovering
this.

00:13:00.540 --> 00:13:05.580
Two hundred and thirty million dollar tax
fraud that these criminals and corrupt officials

00:13:05.580 --> 00:13:07.480
committed.

00:13:07.480 --> 00:13:10.630
The Magnitsky Act was put in place in the
United States and other countries, which freezes

00:13:10.630 --> 00:13:14.990
the assets and bans the visas of Russian human
rights violators.

00:13:14.990 --> 00:13:20.340
Putin got so angry about the Magnitsky Act
that he banned the adoption of Russian orphans

00:13:20.340 --> 00:13:22.170
by American families.

00:13:22.170 --> 00:13:28.510
He made it the single largest foreign policy
priority to repeal the Magnitsky Act.

00:13:28.510 --> 00:13:33.320
Even since an Italian vessel, Niscayah, Russian
lawyer to Trump Tower to meet with Donald

00:13:33.320 --> 00:13:38.150
Trump Jr., Paul Manafort and Jared Kushner
before Trump was elected to say if he's elected,

00:13:38.150 --> 00:13:39.150
please were.

00:13:39.150 --> 00:13:41.670
The Magnitsky Act.

00:13:41.670 --> 00:13:48.590
But here's here's the crux after the Magnitsky
Act was passed, he he got he had lost his

00:13:48.590 --> 00:13:53.260
he gets so upset with me that my name is now
mentioned by Vladimir Putin.

00:13:53.260 --> 00:13:58.360
And in fact, he mentioned it very profoundly
when he met with Trump and Putin, met with

00:13:58.360 --> 00:14:02.120
Trump at the Helsinki Summit.

00:14:02.120 --> 00:14:06.750
And at the end of the summit, there was this
question about whether the US, whether Russia

00:14:06.750 --> 00:14:11.560
would hand over the 12 GRU officers who hacked
the election.

00:14:11.560 --> 00:14:16.520
And Putin said we will hand them over if Trump
hands over Bill Browder.

00:14:16.520 --> 00:14:19.680
Speaker 1: Bill, I so appreciate your time
today.

00:14:19.680 --> 00:14:21.660
The book is Red Notice.

00:14:21.660 --> 00:14:27.040
Bill Browder is the CEO of Hermitage Capital
Management and also head of the Global Magnitsky

00:14:27.040 --> 00:14:28.920
Justice Campaign.

00:14:28.920 --> 00:14:29.920
Really appreciate your time today.

00:14:29.920 --> 00:14:30.920
Speaker 4: My pleasure.

00:14:30.920 --> 00:14:30.924
Great to see you.

